1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,348
Act one is written, and a table has played it.

2
00:00:05,248 --> 00:00:09,161
Now it has to work in somebody else's house.

3
00:00:10,961 --> 00:00:16,178
You've sent a mission before, at the end of the first class.

4
00:00:17,078 --> 00:00:19,252
A campaign's the same job,

5
00:00:19,702 --> 00:00:21,876
with four differences that matter.

6
00:00:23,076 --> 00:00:24,815
A mission's played once.

7
00:00:25,265 --> 00:00:28,743
A campaign's played for months, from a save.

8
00:00:30,543 --> 00:00:32,283
A mission's sent once.

9
00:00:32,733 --> 00:00:37,080
A campaign's sent again, every time you finish an act.

10
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:41,924
The title of a mission can change.

11
00:00:42,824 --> 00:00:47,607
The title of a campaign names the save, so it can't.

12
00:00:49,407 --> 00:00:52,450
And your notes are in the folder,

13
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:55,509
with the whole plot in them.

14
00:00:56,709 --> 00:00:58,883
First, say what this is.

15
00:00:59,783 --> 00:01:02,391
One line in the description file.

16
00:01:02,841 --> 00:01:07,624
A campaign for one ship, act one, five evenings, version one.

17
00:01:09,424 --> 00:01:12,902
No hyphen in that line, and no colon.

18
00:01:13,802 --> 00:01:18,585
That's the one mistake that can stop the game for everybody,

19
00:01:19,035 --> 00:01:21,643
and lint checks it for you.

20
00:01:23,443 --> 00:01:25,617
And leave the name alone.

21
00:01:26,067 --> 00:01:28,241
From the first evening on,

22
00:01:28,691 --> 00:01:32,170
that name is where the crew's save is.

23
00:01:33,370 --> 00:01:37,283
Next, the folder gets a name of its own.

24
00:01:39,083 --> 00:01:42,126
I measured two things about that name.

25
00:01:43,026 --> 00:01:45,635
Nothing inside the files holds it.

26
00:01:47,435 --> 00:01:50,478
And the save doesn't follow the folder.

27
00:01:50,928 --> 00:01:52,667
It follows the title.

28
00:01:54,467 --> 00:01:57,946
So renaming the folder doesn't lose your save.

29
00:01:58,846 --> 00:02:01,454
But two folders with one title

30
00:02:01,904 --> 00:02:04,078
are playing the same campaign,

31
00:02:04,528 --> 00:02:07,137
whether you meant that or not.

32
00:02:08,337 --> 00:02:11,815
Then the three checks, in the usual order.

33
00:02:13,615 --> 00:02:16,224
I run lint, and it's clean.

34
00:02:17,124 --> 00:02:18,863
Then I run doctor,

35
00:02:19,313 --> 00:02:22,357
and the last block is your folder.

36
00:02:24,157 --> 00:02:27,635
Twenty-one libraries, where your first mission had twelve.

37
00:02:28,535 --> 00:02:30,274
That's the universe machinery.

38
00:02:32,074 --> 00:02:35,552
And then the walk, on a deleted save.

39
00:02:36,452 --> 00:02:39,930
And delete the save again when you're done.

40
00:02:41,130 --> 00:02:43,739
Now the step a campaign adds.

41
00:02:44,639 --> 00:02:46,813
Your own pages come out.

42
00:02:48,613 --> 00:02:52,526
The campaign page is every act, and the ending.

43
00:02:53,426 --> 00:02:56,904
The playtest page is what other crews did.

44
00:02:58,704 --> 00:03:01,748
And any save you kept in here

45
00:03:02,198 --> 00:03:04,807
has every hidden step in it.

46
00:03:06,607 --> 00:03:09,215
Then delete what the tools made.

47
00:03:09,665 --> 00:03:11,404
The printed bible especially.

48
00:03:13,204 --> 00:03:14,943
Seven files are left.

49
00:03:15,843 --> 00:03:18,017
Those seven are the mission.

50
00:03:19,217 --> 00:03:20,957
Zip the folder itself,

51
00:03:21,407 --> 00:03:24,885
the way you did in the first class.

52
00:03:26,685 --> 00:03:28,424
One honest word here.

53
00:03:29,324 --> 00:03:34,107
The universe file is plain text, and it's the whole act.

54
00:03:35,007 --> 00:03:38,920
A host who opens it has read your campaign.

55
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:43,328
That's what a host is for.

56
00:03:44,228 --> 00:03:48,576
It's the same as handing someone the adventure to run.

57
00:03:50,376 --> 00:03:55,159
It also means you can't be crew in your own campaign

58
00:03:55,609 --> 00:03:59,087
unless somebody else is willing to read it.

59
00:04:00,287 --> 00:04:03,765
The note is where a campaign really differs.

60
00:04:05,565 --> 00:04:09,043
The setup's the same three commands as before.

61
00:04:09,943 --> 00:04:12,987
Then four things only a campaign needs.

62
00:04:14,787 --> 00:04:17,830
Start it the same way every week,

63
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:20,454
and never choose new game.

64
00:04:21,354 --> 00:04:23,093
Don't rename the ship.

65
00:04:23,993 --> 00:04:26,602
End each evening by jumping home.

66
00:04:27,502 --> 00:04:31,415
And copy the save file somewhere safe, every week.

67
00:04:32,315 --> 00:04:36,228
The note gives its name, and where it lives.

68
00:04:37,428 --> 00:04:41,341
When act two's ready, you send the folder again,

69
00:04:41,791 --> 00:04:43,965
and there are three rules.

70
00:04:45,765 --> 00:04:49,243
The title doesn't change, not by a letter.

71
00:04:50,143 --> 00:04:54,057
Nothing the crew has met is deleted or renamed,

72
00:04:54,507 --> 00:04:56,680
because you only ever add.

73
00:04:58,480 --> 00:05:02,393
And act one's ending gets its one new line,

74
00:05:02,843 --> 00:05:07,191
the one that shows the first lead of act two.

75
00:05:08,991 --> 00:05:11,165
The host swaps the folder.

76
00:05:12,065 --> 00:05:14,239
The save isn't in it,

77
00:05:14,689 --> 00:05:16,863
so the crew carries on.

78
00:05:18,663 --> 00:05:22,141
And before you send it, check it yourself.

79
00:05:22,591 --> 00:05:28,678
Start the new folder on your own save from the end of act one,

80
00:05:29,128 --> 00:05:33,041
and see that the first new lead is there.

81
00:05:34,241 --> 00:05:35,980
So that's a release.

82
00:05:36,430 --> 00:05:40,343
A named folder, a clean zip, and a note.

83
00:05:42,143 --> 00:05:44,752
The capstone's next, when it's written.

84
00:05:45,652 --> 00:05:48,261
And everything it'll ask of you

85
00:05:48,711 --> 00:05:51,320
is already on your campaign page.
